var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"For complete workflows, start with the user guide. This page lists the available types and functions.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","text":"Modules = [LanceDB]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.ArrowSchema","page":"API reference","text":"ArrowSchema\n\nJulia mirror of the Arrow C Data Interface FFI_ArrowSchema struct. You rarely need to construct one directly — use make_schema or make_vector_schema instead, and free with release_arrow_schema.\n\nExported so that users can pass an Ptr{ArrowSchema} to the low-level create_table(conn, name, schema::Ptr{ArrowSchema}) overload when they need full control over the schema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.ArrowSchema"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.BinaryColumn","page":"API reference","text":"BinaryColumn(values; large=false)\n\nMark a column of byte vectors (optionally missing) as Arrow Binary rather than a numeric list. Use large=true for 64-bit offsets (LargeBinary). Values are borrowed during synchronous ingestion; do not mutate them then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.BinaryColumn"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.Connection","page":"API reference","text":"Connection\n\nManages a LanceDB connection. Freed automatically by the GC via finalizer; use the do-block form for deterministic cleanup.\n\nopen(Connection, \"./mydb\") do conn\n    ...\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.Connection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.DistanceType","page":"API reference","text":"DistanceType\n\nDistance metric used for vector search.\n\nL2      — Euclidean (L2) distance (default)\nCosine  — cosine similarity (good for normalized embeddings)\nDot     — dot-product distance\nHamming — Hamming distance (binary vectors)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.DistanceType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.IDDataset","page":"API reference","text":"IDDataset(table; id_column=:id, ids=nothing, columns=nothing, batch_size=256)\n\nA borrowed table plus an in-memory manifest of unique application IDs. Without ids, scans only the ID column once (not media/embeddings). ds[i] returns one named-tuple row; ds[indices] returns a column table. Uses 1-based Julia indices. Keep the table open and unchanged for the dataset's lifetime. Explicit ids are checked for uniqueness; their existence is checked when fetched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.IDDataset"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.IndexType","page":"API reference","text":"IndexType\n\nType of index to create.\n\nVector index types (used with create_vector_index):\n\nAuto      — let LanceDB choose (default)\nIVFFlat   — inverted file with flat quantization\nIVFPQ     — inverted file with product quantization\nIVFHNSWpq — IVF + HNSW + product quantization\nIVFHNSWsq — IVF + HNSW + scalar quantization\n\nScalar index types (used with create_scalar_index):\n\nBTree     — B-tree index for range/equality queries (default)\nBitmap    — bitmap index for low-cardinality columns\nLabelList — index for list-typed columns\n\nFull-text index (used with create_fts_index):\n\nFTS       — full-text search index\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.IndexType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBException","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBException(code, message)\n\nThrown whenever a LanceDB C API call returns an error. code is the raw LanceDBError integer; message is the human-readable string returned by the C library (or a fallback when none is provided).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBException"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBExpr","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBExpr\n\nWraps a DataFusion expression handle. Expressions are consumed (transferred to the C side) when passed to binary operators or query filters, so they cannot be reused after that point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBExpr"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBFtsIndexConfig","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBFtsIndexConfig()\n\nConfiguration for create_fts_index.\n\nFields\n\nbase_tokenizer   — tokenizer name (C_NULL → \"simple\")\nlanguage         — language for stemming/stop-words (C_NULL → \"English\")\nmax_token_length — maximum token length; -1 means no limit\nlowercase        — 1 to lowercase tokens before indexing (default)\nstem             — 1 to apply stemming\nremove_stop_words— 1 to drop common stop words\nascii_folding    — 1 to normalize accented characters to ASCII\nreplace          — 1 to replace an existing index\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBFtsIndexConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBMergeInsertConfig","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBMergeInsertConfig()\n\nConfiguration for merge_insert (upsert). Both flags default to 1, which is the standard upsert behaviour.\n\nFields\n\nwhen_matched_update_all    — 1 to overwrite every column of a matching row\nwhen_not_matched_insert_all— 1 to insert rows whose key is not found\nwhen_matched_update_all_condition — optional C string containing a SQL condition\nwhen_matched_update_all_expr — optional expression handle (alternative to SQL)\n\nBoth condition pointers default to NULL. If setting either pointer directly, keep its owning string or expression alive with GC.@preserve for the entire merge_insert call. The expression is borrowed, not consumed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBMergeInsertConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBScalarIndexConfig","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBScalarIndexConfig()\n\nConfiguration for create_scalar_index.\n\nFields\n\nreplace                  — 1 to replace an existing index\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBScalarIndexConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBSessionOptions","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBSessionOptions()\n\nSession-level cache configuration passed to Connection.\n\nFields\n\nindex_cache_bytes    — maximum bytes for the vector index cache; 0 uses the library default\nmetadata_cache_bytes — maximum bytes for the metadata cache; 0 uses the library default\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBSessionOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.LanceDBVectorIndexConfig","page":"API reference","text":"LanceDBVectorIndexConfig()\n\nConfiguration for create_vector_index. Construct with the zero-argument constructor and mutate the fields you want to override before passing to create_vector_index.\n\nFields\n\nnum_partitions  — number of IVF partitions; default -1 (auto, ≈ √n)\nnum_sub_vectors — number of PQ sub-vectors (PQ/HNSW variants); default -1 (auto)\nmax_iterations  — k-means training iterations; default -1 (auto)\nsample_rate     — fraction of rows used for training; 0.0 means auto\ndistance_type   — DistanceType enum value, default L2\nreplace         — 1 to replace an existing index, 0 to error if one exists\n\ncfg = LanceDBVectorIndexConfig()\ncfg.num_partitions = 16\ncreate_vector_index(tbl, \"embedding\"; type=IVFFlat, config=cfg)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.LanceDBVectorIndexConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.ListColumn","page":"API reference","text":"ListColumn(values; large=false)\n\nMark a column of vectors as variable-length Arrow lists, including empty lists. Unwrapped vectors retain fixed-size embedding semantics. Element types must be concrete enough to infer the child schema, even for an empty column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.ListColumn"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.OptimizeType","page":"API reference","text":"OptimizeType\n\nControls which optimization pass optimize runs.\n\nOptimizeAll     — run all optimizations (default)\nOptimizeCompact — compact small files into larger ones\nOptimizePrune   — delete files belonging to old versions\nOptimizeIndex   — re-index rows added since the last index build\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.OptimizeType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.Query","page":"API reference","text":"Query\n\nBuilder for full-table scans. Chainable methods mutate the query in place (unlike the C builder pattern, which would consume and return a new pointer).\n\nresult = query(tbl) |> filter_where(\"year > 2020\") |> limit(100) |> execute\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.Query"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.QueryResult","page":"API reference","text":"QueryResult\n\nHolds a completed LanceDB query result. Implements the Tables.jl interface so it integrates directly with DataFrames.jl, CSV.jl, etc.\n\nData is materialised on first access via the Arrow C Data Interface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.QueryResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.Session","page":"API reference","text":"Session(; index_cache_bytes=0, metadata_cache_bytes=0)\n\nShare native caches between connections with Connection(path; session=s). Zero selects the native default. Close the session when no longer needed; connections retain their own native reference to its caches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.Session"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.Table","page":"API reference","text":"Table\n\nAn open LanceDB table handle. Freed automatically by the GC via finalizer; call close(tbl) for deterministic cleanup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.Table"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.TableSink","page":"API reference","text":"TableSink(conn, name)\n\nA write target for the Tables.jl sink protocol. Pipe any Tables.jl-compatible source into Tables.materializer(TableSink(conn, name)) to create a new table:\n\nCSV.File(\"data.csv\")  |> Tables.materializer(TableSink(conn, \"mytable\"))\nArrow.Table(buf)      |> Tables.materializer(TableSink(conn, \"embeddings\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.TableSink"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LanceDB.VectorQuery","page":"API reference","text":"VectorQuery\n\nBuilder for ANN (approximate nearest-neighbour) search.\n\nresult = vector_search(tbl, query_vec, \"embedding\") |>\n         distance_type(Cosine) |>\n         nprobes(20) |>\n         limit(10) |>\n         execute\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.VectorQuery"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.add-Tuple{Table, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"add(tbl, data)\n\nAppend rows from any Tables.jl-compatible source (NamedTuple, DataFrame, …).\n\nSupported column element types: Int8/16/32/64, UInt8/16/32/64, Float32/64, Bool, AbstractString (UTF-8), and fixed-length nested vectors (FixedSizeList). Nullable columns and nullable list elements are supported, along with explicit BinaryColumn/ListColumn inputs, nested NamedTuple records and Date/DateTime/Time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.add"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.add-Tuple{Table, Ptr{LanceDB.LanceDBRecordBatchReaderHandle}}","page":"API reference","text":"add(tbl, reader)\n\nAppend data using a raw LanceDBRecordBatchReaderHandle (consumed by this call).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.add"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.append_partitions!-Tuple{Table, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"append_partitions!(table, source)\n\nAppend each Tables.partitions(source) batch separately and return table. Only one input partition is converted at a time. Each append commits its own version: a failure leaves earlier partitions committed (not an atomic transaction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.append_partitions!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.array_has-Tuple{LanceDBExpr, LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"array_has(array_expr, value_expr) builds an array-membership predicate; both inputs are consumed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.array_has"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.batches-Tuple{IDDataset}","page":"API reference","text":"batches(dataset; batchsize=32, shuffle=false, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n        drop_last=false, transform=identity)\n\nLazily fetch one training batch at a time. Shuffle applies to the ID manifest; construct a new iterator for each shuffled epoch. transform receives a column table and may decode media, augment samples or return model-ready arrays. No background worker or hidden shared-handle concurrency is started.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.batches"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.cache_stats-Tuple{Session}","page":"API reference","text":"cache_stats(session; cache=:index) returns cache hits, misses, entries and bytes. Use cache=:metadata for the metadata cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.cache_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.col-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"col(name) -> LanceDBExpr\n\nCreate a column reference expression. Use this as the left-hand side of comparisons in the expression DSL:\n\ncol(\"year\") > lit(2020)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.col"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.count_rows-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"count_rows(tbl) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.count_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.create_fts_index-Tuple{Table, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"create_fts_index(tbl, column; config=LanceDBFtsIndexConfig())\ncreate_fts_index(tbl, columns::Vector{String}; ...)\n\nBuild a full-text search (FTS) index on a UTF-8 string column. The index is named ${column}_idx. The current C API supports creating the index but does not expose a full-text query builder. Tokenization options (language, stemming, stop words, etc.) are set via LanceDBFtsIndexConfig.\n\ncreate_fts_index(tbl, \"title\")\n\ncfg = LanceDBFtsIndexConfig()\ncfg.stem = 1\ncfg.remove_stop_words = 1\ncreate_fts_index(tbl, \"body\"; config=cfg)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.create_fts_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.create_scalar_index-Tuple{Table, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"create_scalar_index(tbl, column; type=BTree, config=LanceDBScalarIndexConfig())\ncreate_scalar_index(tbl, columns::Vector{String}; ...)\n\nBuild a scalar index on column to accelerate SQL WHERE filters on that column. BTree (default) suits range and equality queries on numeric or string columns. Use Bitmap for low-cardinality columns and LabelList for list-typed columns.\n\ncreate_scalar_index(tbl, \"year\")\ncreate_scalar_index(tbl, \"category\"; type=Bitmap)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.create_scalar_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.create_table-Tuple{Connection, AbstractString, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"create_table(conn, name, data)\n\nCreate a table and populate it from any Tables.jl-compatible source. The schema is inferred from the data column types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.create_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.create_table-Tuple{Connection, AbstractString, Ptr{ArrowSchema}}","page":"API reference","text":"create_table(conn, name, schema; reader=C_NULL) -> Table\n\nCreate a new table with the given Arrow C ABI schema pointer. Pass a LanceDBRecordBatchReaderHandle pointer in reader to populate with initial data; leave as C_NULL to create an empty table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.create_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.create_vector_index-Tuple{Table, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"create_vector_index(tbl, column; type=Auto, config=LanceDBVectorIndexConfig())\ncreate_vector_index(tbl, columns::Vector{String}; ...)\n\nBuild a vector index on column (or a list of columns) to accelerate ANN search. The default type=Auto lets LanceDB pick the algorithm; specify IVFFlat, IVFPQ, IVFHNSWpq, or IVFHNSWsq explicitly when needed.\n\nTraining requirements depend on the index type and its configuration. After adding new rows call optimize(tbl; type=OptimizeIndex) to index the delta.\n\ncfg = LanceDBVectorIndexConfig()\ncfg.num_partitions = 16\ncreate_vector_index(tbl, \"embedding\"; type=IVFFlat, config=cfg)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.create_vector_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.delete_metadata!-Tuple{Table, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"delete_metadata!(table, keys) removes metadata keys; missing keys are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.delete_metadata!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.delete_rows-Tuple{Table, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"delete_rows(tbl, predicate)\n\nDelete rows matching the SQL predicate string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.delete_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.delete_rows-Tuple{Table, LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"delete_rows(table, expr::LanceDBExpr) deletes matching rows, consuming the expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.delete_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.distance_type-Tuple{VectorQuery, DistanceType}","page":"API reference","text":"distance_type(vq, dt::DistanceType) -> VectorQuery\ndistance_type(dt::DistanceType)     -> Function\n\nSet the distance metric for the search. Defaults to L2. Available values: L2, Cosine, Dot, Hamming. The single-argument form is curried for |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.distance_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.drop_all_tables-Tuple{Connection}","page":"API reference","text":"drop_all_tables(conn; namespace=nothing) deletes all tables in the specified namespace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.drop_all_tables"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.drop_index-Tuple{Table, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"drop_index(tbl, name)\n\nDelete the index named name from tbl. Throws LanceDBException if the index does not exist. Use list_indices(tbl) to find valid names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.drop_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.drop_table-Tuple{Connection, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"drop_table(conn, name)\n\nDrop a table. Throws LanceDBException on failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.drop_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.ef-Tuple{VectorQuery, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"ef(vq, n) -> VectorQuery\nef(n)     -> Function\n\nSet the HNSW ef (size of the dynamic candidate list during search). Higher values improve recall; lower values are faster. Only relevant when an HNSW-based index is in use. The single-argument form is curried for |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.ef"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.embedding_search-Tuple{Table, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"embedding_search(table, input, encoder; column=:embedding)\n\nEncode one query (text, bytes, image, etc.) with the same batched encoder protocol as with_embeddings, then return a chainable VectorQuery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.embedding_search"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.execute-Tuple{Query}","page":"API reference","text":"execute(q) -> QueryResult\n\nRun the query. Consumes the query handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.execute"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.execute-Tuple{VectorQuery}","page":"API reference","text":"execute(vq::VectorQuery) -> QueryResult\n\nRun the vector search and materialise the result. Consumes the query; calling execute again on the same object will throw LanceDBException.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.execute"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.explain_plan-Tuple{Union{Query, VectorQuery}}","page":"API reference","text":"explain_plan(query; verbose=false) returns a plan without consuming the query.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.explain_plan"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.filter_expr-Tuple{Query, LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"filter_expr(q, expr) -> Query\n\nSet a DataFusion LanceDBExpr filter. The expr handle is consumed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.filter_expr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.filter_expr-Tuple{VectorQuery, LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"filter_expr(vq::VectorQuery, expr::LanceDBExpr) -> VectorQuery\n\nApply a DataFusion LanceDBExpr filter to the vector search results. The expression handle is consumed. See also the curried form from Query: filter_expr(expr) works with |> on both Query and VectorQuery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.filter_expr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.filter_where-Tuple{Query, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"filter_where(q, predicate) -> Query\n\nSet a SQL WHERE predicate. If a DataFusion expression is also set it takes precedence (see filter_expr).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.filter_where"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.filter_where-Tuple{VectorQuery, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"filter_where(vq::VectorQuery, predicate) -> VectorQuery\n\nApply a SQL WHERE predicate to the vector search results. Only rows matching predicate are returned even if they would rank in the top-k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.filter_where"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.get_metadata-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"get_metadata(table; keys=nothing) returns table metadata as a Dict{String,String}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.get_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.index_stats-Tuple{Table, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"index_stats(tbl, name) -> LanceDBIndexStats\n\nReturn statistics for the index named name. The returned struct has three fields:\n\nnum_indexed_rows   — rows covered by the index\nnum_unindexed_rows — rows added since the last index build (the \"delta\")\nnum_indices        — number of index fragments\n\nCall optimize(tbl; type=OptimizeIndex) to reduce num_unindexed_rows to zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.index_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.isin-Tuple{LanceDBExpr, Vararg{LanceDBExpr}}","page":"API reference","text":"isin(expr, values...) -> LanceDBExpr\n\nBuild an IN-list predicate. expr and all values are consumed.\n\nisin(col(\"label\"), lit(\"cat\"), lit(\"dog\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.isin"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.isnotnull-Tuple{LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"isnotnull(e::LanceDBExpr) -> LanceDBExpr\n\nBuild an IS NOT NULL predicate. e is consumed.\n\nquery(tbl) |> filter_expr(isnotnull(col(\"title\"))) |> execute\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.isnotnull"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.isnull-Tuple{LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"isnull(e::LanceDBExpr) -> LanceDBExpr\n\nBuild an IS NULL predicate. e is consumed.\n\nfilter_expr(col(\"title\") |> isnull)     # rows where title IS NULL\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.isnull"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.json_array_has-Tuple{LanceDBExpr, AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, LanceDBExpr}","page":"API reference","text":"json_array_has(expr, path, value; quote_value=true) builds a JSON array membership predicate. path is a vector of string keys. Both expressions are consumed; set quote_value=false for numeric or boolean JSON literals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.json_array_has"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LanceDB.json_contains","page":"API reference","text":"Test existence of a JSON key path. Consumes expr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.json_contains"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LanceDB.json_get_bool","page":"API reference","text":"Extract a Bool at a JSON key path. Consumes expr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.json_get_bool"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LanceDB.json_get_float","page":"API reference","text":"Extract a Float64 at a JSON key path. Consumes expr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.json_get_float"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LanceDB.json_get_int","page":"API reference","text":"Extract an Int64 at a JSON key path. Consumes expr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.json_get_int"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LanceDB.json_get_str","page":"API reference","text":"Extract a string at a JSON key path. Consumes expr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.json_get_str"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.limit-Tuple{Query, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"limit(q, n) -> Query\nlimit(n)    -> Function\n\nCap the number of rows returned. The single-argument form returns a curried function suitable for use with |>:\n\nquery(tbl) |> limit(10) |> execute\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.limit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.limit-Tuple{VectorQuery, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"limit(vq::VectorQuery, n) -> VectorQuery\n\nReturn at most n nearest neighbours. The single-argument curried form also works: vector_search(tbl, vec, \"col\") |> limit(10) |> execute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.limit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.list_indices-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"list_indices(tbl) -> Vector{String}\n\nReturn the names of all indexes on tbl. Index names follow the pattern ${column}_idx (e.g. \"embedding_idx\", \"year_idx\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.list_indices"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.list_namespaces-Tuple{Connection}","page":"API reference","text":"list_namespaces(conn; parent=nothing) lists namespaces supported by the backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.list_namespaces"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.list_versions-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"list_versions(table)\n\nReturn a vector of named tuples (a Tables.jl row table). Timestamps retain the native UTC Unix seconds and subsecond nanoseconds without rounding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.list_versions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.lit-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"lit(v) -> LanceDBExpr\n\nWrap a Julia scalar as a literal expression. Supported types: AbstractString, Integer (stored as Int64), AbstractFloat (stored as Float64), Bool.\n\nlit(\"Dune\")      # string literal\nlit(2021)        # integer literal\nlit(7.5f0)       # float literal (promoted to Float64)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.lit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.make_schema-Tuple{Vector{Pair{String, String}}}","page":"API reference","text":"make_schema(fields) -> Ptr{ArrowSchema}\n\nAllocate a root Arrow C ABI schema (struct type) from a list of (name, format_string) pairs. Caller is responsible for releasing it via release_arrow_schema.\n\nCommon format strings\n\n\"u\"      — UTF-8 string\n\"l\"      — Int64\n\"i\"      — Int32\n\"f\"      — Float32\n\"g\"      — Float64\n\"+w:N\"   — FixedSizeList of N elements (add a child for the element type)\n\"+s\"     — Struct (set children manually)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.make_schema"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.make_vector_schema-Tuple{String, String, Int64}","page":"API reference","text":"make_vector_schema(key_field, vec_field, dim) -> Ptr{ArrowSchema}\n\nConvenience builder for the canonical LanceDB test schema: {key_field: utf8, vec_field: FixedSizeList<Float32>[dim]}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.make_vector_schema"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.merge_insert-Tuple{Table, Any, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"merge_insert(tbl, data, on_columns; config=LanceDBMergeInsertConfig())\n\nUpsert rows from any Tables.jl-compatible source, keyed on on_columns. Matched rows are updated; unmatched rows are inserted. condition optionally restricts updates using a SQL string or a borrowed LanceDBExpr. Refer to columns with target. and source. prefixes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.merge_insert"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.merge_insert-Tuple{Table, Ptr{LanceDB.LanceDBRecordBatchReaderHandle}, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"merge_insert(tbl, reader, on_columns; config=LanceDBMergeInsertConfig())\n\nUpsert rows keyed on on_columns. The reader is consumed by this call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.merge_insert"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.notiin-Tuple{LanceDBExpr, Vararg{LanceDBExpr}}","page":"API reference","text":"notiin(expr, values...) -> LanceDBExpr\n\nBuild a NOT IN-list predicate. expr and all values are consumed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.notiin"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.nprobes-Tuple{VectorQuery, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"nprobes(vq, n) -> VectorQuery\nnprobes(n)     -> Function\n\nSet the number of IVF partitions probed during an indexed ANN search. Higher values improve recall at the cost of speed. Only effective when an IVF-based vector index exists; ignored for flat (un-indexed) search. The single-argument form is curried for |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.nprobes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.offset-Tuple{Query, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"offset(q, n) -> Query\noffset(n)    -> Function\n\nSkip the first n rows. Combine with limit for pagination:\n\nquery(tbl) |> offset(20) |> limit(10) |> execute\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.open_table-Tuple{Connection, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"open_table(conn, name) -> Table\n\nOpen an existing table. Throws LanceDBException if the table does not exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.open_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.optimize-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"optimize(tbl; type=OptimizeAll)\n\nCompact files and/or prune old versions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.optimize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.query-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"query(tbl) -> Query\n\nStart a full-table scan on tbl. Chain builder methods before calling execute to materialise results:\n\nusing DataFrames\n\nresult = query(tbl) |> filter_where(\"year > 2020\") |> limit(100) |> execute\ndf     = DataFrame(result)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.query"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.refine_factor-Tuple{VectorQuery, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"refine_factor(vq, k) -> VectorQuery\nrefine_factor(k)     -> Function\n\nAfter the ANN search returns candidates, fetch k × limit raw rows and re-rank them by exact distance. Increases accuracy at the cost of extra I/O. A value of 1 (the default) disables re-ranking. The single-argument form is curried for |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.refine_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.release_arrow_schema-Tuple{Ptr{ArrowSchema}}","page":"API reference","text":"release_arrow_schema(schema)\n\nFree all memory allocated by make_schema / make_vector_schema. Must be called exactly once after the schema is no longer needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.release_arrow_schema"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.rename_table-Tuple{Connection, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"rename_table(conn, old, new; current_namespace=nothing, new_namespace=nothing). Currently supported by the upstream Cloud backend only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.rename_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.reopen!-Tuple{Connection}","page":"API reference","text":"reopen!(conn::Connection) -> Connection\n\nRe-establish a connection that was previously closed with close. Uses the URI and options captured at construction time. Returns conn unchanged if it is already open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.reopen!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.sample_rows-Tuple{IDDataset, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"sample_rows(dataset, n; replace=false, rng=Random.default_rng()) samples uniformly over the dataset's ID manifest and returns a column table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.sample_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.select_cols-Tuple{Query, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"select_cols(q, cols) -> Query\nselect_cols(cols)    -> Function\n\nRestrict the returned columns to cols (a Vector{String}). Columns not listed are omitted from the QueryResult. The single-argument form is curried for use with |>:\n\nquery(tbl) |> select_cols([\"id\", \"title\"]) |> execute\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.select_cols"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.select_cols-Tuple{VectorQuery, Vector{String}}","page":"API reference","text":"select_cols(vq::VectorQuery, cols) -> VectorQuery\n\nRestrict the returned columns to cols. The _distance column is always included even if not listed. The curried select_cols(cols) form works with |> on both Query and VectorQuery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.select_cols"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.set_metadata!-Tuple{Table, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"set_metadata!(table, pairs) inserts or updates string metadata, returning the table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.set_metadata!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.table_names-Tuple{Connection}","page":"API reference","text":"table_names(conn) -> Vector{String}\n\nList all table names in the database.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.table_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.table_schema-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"table_schema(table)\n\nReturn the Arrow schema as a Julia tree of named tuples (name, format, nullable, children, dictionary). Field metadata and extension metadata are not included. Physical type descriptions remain available for types not yet supported by the Julia data converter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.table_schema"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.table_version-Tuple{Table}","page":"API reference","text":"table_version(tbl) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.table_version"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.take_ids-Tuple{Table, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"take_ids(table, ids; id_column=:id, columns=nothing, batch_size=256,\n         on_missing=:error, check_version=true)\n\nFetch rows by unique application ID using indexed predicates. Returns a Tables.jl column table in requested order, including repeated IDs. Create a scalar index on the ID column for efficient lookup. This is not native take_offsets/take_row_ids. Duplicate stored IDs are rejected. on_missing=:skip omits missing IDs. Each native query is limited to a bounded number of distinct IDs. The complete returned result still occupies memory. Version checks detect changes visible to this handle; they do not provide snapshot isolation against external writers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.take_ids"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.uri-Tuple{Connection}","page":"API reference","text":"uri(conn) -> String\n\nReturn the URI this connection points to. Works even after the connection has been closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.uri"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.vector_search-Tuple{Table, Vector{Float32}, String}","page":"API reference","text":"vector_search(tbl, vec, column) -> VectorQuery\nvector_search(tbl, vec)         -> VectorQuery\n\nStart an approximate nearest-neighbour (ANN) search for rows whose column embedding is closest to vec. Chain builder methods before calling execute:\n\nusing DataFrames\n\ndf = DataFrame(\n    vector_search(tbl, Float32[0.1, 0.8, 0.3], \"embedding\") |>\n    distance_type(Cosine) |>\n    limit(5) |>\n    execute\n)\nprintln(df._distance)   # ascending L2/cosine distances\n\nThe result always includes a _distance column with the computed distances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.vector_search"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#LanceDB.with_embeddings-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"with_embeddings(data, source, encoder; column=:embedding, batchsize=64, overwrite=false)\n\nReturn a Tables.jl column table with generated Float32 embeddings. encoder accepts a batch of source values and returns a vector of vectors or a matrix whose columns are embeddings. Existing columns are reused where possible. Encoding/decoding/model installation is supplied by the caller; nothing is downloaded automatically. Empty input cannot infer a vector dimension. For large inputs, apply this function to each Tables.jl partition before append.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LanceDB.with_embeddings"},{"category":"section","location":"capabilities/#Supported-features-and-usage-notes","page":"Capabilities and ownership","text":"This package uses lancedb-c 0.33. The available operations are summarized below; see the examples in the user guide and multimodal guide.\n\nTask Functions\nOpen a database Connection, open(Connection, ...) do\nManage tables create_table, open_table, table_names, drop_table\nWrite and update rows add, merge_insert, delete_rows\nFilter and select query, filter_where, filter_expr, select_cols, limit, offset\nSearch embeddings vector_search, embedding_search\nManage indexes create_scalar_index, create_vector_index, list_indices, index_stats, optimize\nRead results DataFrame(result), eachrow(df)\nStore media and metadata BinaryColumn, ListColumn, nested NamedTuples\nBuild training batches take_ids, IDDataset, batches, sample_rows, with_embeddings\nInspect a table table_schema, table_version, get_metadata, list_versions\nReuse caches Session, cache_stats","title":"Supported features and usage notes"},{"category":"section","location":"capabilities/#Closing-resources","page":"Capabilities and ownership","text":"Use open blocks for databases and existing tables:\n\nusing LanceDB, DataFrames\n\nopen(Connection, \"./my-database\") do db\n    open(Table, db, \"items\") do table\n        result = execute(query(table) |> limit(10))\n        try\n            rows = DataFrame(result)\n        finally\n            close(result)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nClose newly created tables and unused queries with close. Julia result columns remain valid after closing the result. Executing a query consumes it; start another query to run again. Expressions are also consumed, so use copy(expr) before reusing one. Avoid operating on the same handle from concurrent tasks.","title":"Closing resources"},{"category":"section","location":"capabilities/#Large-datasets","page":"Capabilities and ownership","text":"Use append_partitions! to append input batches and batches(dataset) to fetch training samples incrementally. Tables.partitions(result) avoids joining result batches, but a single execute still collects the full query result internally. Select only needed columns and limit results when memory is constrained.\n\nEach append is a separate commit: an error does not undo earlier batches. A dataset is not a snapshot; keep the source table unchanged while training. On Windows, index optimization can leave native file mappings open until process exit, preventing immediate deletion of the database directory.","title":"Large datasets"},{"category":"section","location":"capabilities/#Current-limitations","page":"Capabilities and ownership","text":"FTS indexes can be created, but full-text queries and hybrid search are not available yet.\nVersion history can be listed, but checkout/restore, schema alteration and general row-update expressions are unavailable.\nVideo bytes can be stored and read, but extracting frames requires a decoder. Lazy Blob/range reads are not available.\nNamespaces and table rename depend on the backend. S3 connection examples are in the user guide; real cloud authentication is not covered by the local test suite.\nSupported input types include integers, Float32/Float64, Bool, strings, missing values, binary/list columns, nested records and Date/DateTime/Time. Decimal and other temporal formats are not supported.\nArrow and DataFrames field metadata are not automatically preserved. Use set_metadata! for table metadata you need to keep.\nA completely empty native query result can have no column names; inspect table_schema(table) when you need the table's schema separately.","title":"Current limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Tables-and-queries","page":"User guide","text":"","title":"Tables and queries"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Write-DataFrames","page":"User guide","text":"create_table and add accept Tables.jl-compatible column and row tables. The package does not require DataFrames.jl, CSV.jl or Arrow.jl at runtime; install them in your own environment when using their table implementations.\n\nusing LanceDB, DataFrames\n\nmktempdir() do path\n    open(Connection, path) do db\n        table = create_table(db, \"items\", DataFrame(id=[1, 2], text=[\"cat\", \"dog\"]))\n        try\n            add(table, DataFrame(id=[3], text=[\"bird\"]))\n            merge_insert(table, DataFrame(id=[2, 4], text=[\"updated dog\", \"fish\"]), :id)\n            delete_rows(table, col(:id) == 4)\n            set_metadata!(table, Dict(\"source\" => \"example\"))\n            println(DataFrame(take_ids(table, [2, 1])))\n        finally\n            close(table)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\u001b[1m2×2 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m id    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m text        \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String      \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────\n   1 │     2  updated dog\n   2 │     1  cat\n\nUse df = DataFrame(result) to obtain a DataFrame, df.id to access a column, and eachrow(df) to iterate rows. NamedTuples and other Tables.jl-compatible inputs are also accepted.\n\nappend_partitions!(table, source) converts and commits each Tables.partitions(source) partition separately. It is not an atomic multi-batch transaction. Tables.partitions(result) avoids an extra concatenation of result batches, but the native C API has already collected the complete query result.","title":"Write DataFrames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Filters-and-projection","page":"User guide","text":"The following independent example also runs during documentation builds.\n\nusing LanceDB, DataFrames\n\nmktempdir() do path\n    open(Connection, path) do db\n        table = create_table(db, \"items\", DataFrame(id=[1, 2, 3], score=[10, 20, 30]))\n        try\n            q = query(table) |>\n                filter_expr(col(:score) >= 20) |>\n                select_cols([:id, :score]) |>\n                limit(10)\n            result = execute(q)\n            try\n                rows = DataFrame(result)\n                @assert sort(rows.id) == [2, 3]\n                println(sort(rows.id))\n            finally\n                close(result)\n            end\n        finally\n            close(table)\n        end\n    end\nend\n[2, 3]\n\nSQL filters are also available: filter_where(q, \"score >= 20\"). Do not interpolate untrusted values into SQL; use typed expressions such as col(:name) == user_input. Combine expressions with &, | and !, using parentheses around each comparison. Expressions are consumed on use; copy(expr) creates an independent handle.\n\noffset and limit support scan pagination, but scans do not promise a stable row order. Use application-ID lookups when requested ordering matters. select_cols selects existing columns, not arbitrary SQL expressions.","title":"Filters and projection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Indexes-and-vector-search","page":"User guide","text":"create_scalar_index(table, :id)  # BTree by default\ncreate_vector_index(table, :embedding; type=IVFFlat,\n                    config=LanceDBVectorIndexConfig(num_partitions=4))\nq = vector_search(table, Float32[1, 0], :embedding) |>\n    nprobes(4) |> limit(10)\nprintln(explain_plan(q))\nresult = execute(q)\n\nThis fragment assumes an existing table with an embedding column and sufficient training rows. Configure the index and query with the same distance metric. nprobes, refine_factor and ef control the corresponding native search parameters. Close result when finished.\n\nUse list_indices, index_stats, drop_index and optimize to manage indexes. create_fts_index can build a full-text index, but the current C ABI does not expose a full-text query builder or native hybrid search.","title":"Indexes and vector search"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Metadata-and-versions","page":"User guide","text":"set_metadata!(table, Dict(\"source\" => \"training-set\"))\nmetadata = get_metadata(table)\nhistory = list_versions(table)\n\nVersion listing does not provide checkout, rollback or snapshot pinning. See Capabilities and ownership for the remaining C API gaps.","title":"Metadata and versions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Object-storage","page":"User guide","text":"Connection passes the database URI and storage_options to the Rust backend. No Python process is involved. The URI must point to a LanceDB database layout, not an arbitrary folder of images or Parquet files.","title":"Object storage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#S3","page":"User guide","text":"This example requires your own existing bucket, table and credentials. It is not executed during documentation builds.\n\nusing LanceDB, DataFrames\n\noptions = Dict(\n    \"aws_region\" => \"us-east-1\",\n    \"aws_access_key_id\" => ENV[\"AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\"],\n    \"aws_secret_access_key\" => ENV[\"AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\"],\n)\nif haskey(ENV, \"AWS_SESSION_TOKEN\")\n    options[\"aws_session_token\"] = ENV[\"AWS_SESSION_TOKEN\"]\nend\n\nopen(Connection, \"s3://my-bucket/my-database\"; storage_options=options) do db\n    open(Table, db, \"samples\") do table\n        rows = DataFrame(take_ids(table, [42, 7]; id_column=:id))\n        println(rows)\n    end\nend\n\nFor an S3-compatible service, set aws_endpoint. A local HTTP test endpoint also needs aws_allow_http => \"true\"; path-style addressing can be selected with aws_virtual_hosted_style_request => \"false\".\n\nThe constructor captures a copy of the options. Changing the original dictionary does not change reopen!; create a new connection to supply different options. reopen! only reconnects a closed connection and leaves an open one unchanged.","title":"S3"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Validation-and-limits","page":"User guide","text":"Use an existing bucket and your own credentials. Cloud authentication and provider-specific behavior are not covered by the local test suite.\n\ns3:// connections read your object storage directly. LanceDB Cloud uses a different service API and is not fully supported by these bindings.\n\nThe current bindings do not expose Python's read-consistency interval or per-table storage-option overrides. Existing handles may not observe external updates immediately. Keep a training dataset unchanged during an epoch.","title":"Validation and limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Video-and-large-values","page":"User guide","text":"Video data is read as complete byte values. Use a video decoder to extract frames, or store pre-extracted frames as individual rows for sampling. Lazy video/Blob range reads are not available yet.\n\nSee the upstream storage guide and Lance Blob guide. Their latest examples may require a newer lancedb-c version than the one supported here.","title":"Video and large values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Optional-integrations","page":"User guide","text":"Arrow is a weak dependency. Install it in your application and load both packages to enable LanceDBArrowExt automatically:\n\nusing LanceDB, Arrow\nsource = Arrow.Table(\"input.arrow\")\ntable = create_table(db, \"imported\", source)\n\nLoading Arrow enables optimized ingestion of supported Arrow columns, including binary values and variable-length lists. Keep input buffers unchanged during the call.\n\nFor an IPC stream, use append_partitions!(table, Arrow.Stream(\"input.arrow\")) to append batches individually. Arbitrary Arrow metadata are not preserved; see usage notes.\n\nDataFrames needs no extension because its table and view types already implement Tables.jl:\n\nusing DataFrames\nframe = DataFrame(id=[1, 2], text=[\"cat\", \"dog\"])\ntable = create_table(db, \"frames\", frame)\nadd(table, DataFrame(id=[3], text=[\"bird\"]))\nresult = execute(query(table))\ntry\n    output = DataFrame(result)\nfinally\n    close(result)\nend\n\nUse Arrow.write(\"output.arrow\", result) to export query results. The values are preserved, but storage types can change; byte vectors may be written as lists of UInt8. Use set_metadata! explicitly for metadata you need in the database.","title":"Optional integrations"},{"category":"section","location":"multimodal/#Multimodal-data-and-ID-based-training-batches","page":"Multimodal data","text":"Store media as bytes, attach metadata and embeddings, then fetch samples in a chosen order or iterate shuffled training batches.","title":"Multimodal data and ID-based training batches"},{"category":"section","location":"multimodal/#Store-bytes-and-typed-metadata","page":"Multimodal data","text":"using LanceDB, DataFrames, Random\n\ndata = DataFrame(\n    id = [101, 205],\n    media = BinaryColumn([UInt8[1, 2, 3], UInt8[4, 5]]), # toy bytes; replace with read(\"cat.jpg\"), etc.\n    tags = ListColumn([[\"cat\", \"outdoor\"], [\"dog\"]]),\n    info = [(width=640, height=480), (width=800, height=600)],\n    embedding = [Float32[1, 0], Float32[0, 1]],\n)\n\npath = mktempdir()\nconn = Connection(path)\ntbl = create_table(conn, \"images\", data)\ncreate_scalar_index(tbl, :id) # ID lookup index, separate from a vector index\n\nBinaryColumn stores byte vectors, including empty or missing values. Use read(\"image.jpg\") to read a file into bytes and large=true for 64-bit offsets.\n\nListColumn stores variable-length lists such as tags. Use ordinary vectors for fixed-length embeddings, NamedTuples for nested records, and Julia Date/DateTime/Time for temporal values.\n\nWhen another package converts your columns, reapply BinaryColumn or ListColumn if their intended storage type is lost.","title":"Store bytes and typed metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"multimodal/#Fetch-in-requested-order","page":"Multimodal data","text":"selected = DataFrame(take_ids(tbl, [205, 101, 205]; columns=[:id, :media]))\n@assert selected.id == [205, 101, 205]\n\nds = IDDataset(tbl; columns=[:id, :media, :tags])\none_row = ds[1]            # a NamedTuple row, Julia's 1-based position in ds.ids\nsome_rows = DataFrame(ds[[2, 1, 2]])\nsampled = DataFrame(sample_rows(ds, 10; replace=true, rng=MersenneTwister(42)))\n\nUse unique, non-missing string or integer IDs. take_ids preserves the requested order and repetitions. Missing IDs raise KeyError; pass on_missing=:skip to omit them.\n\nIDDataset loads the ID list once, not all media. Keep its table open and unchanged during an epoch, and rebuild the dataset after updates. You can pass ids=[...] to train on a subset.\n\nUse batch_size to limit IDs per query. A large media value is still read in full. Repeated IDs can share nested arrays; copy them before changing each occurrence independently.","title":"Fetch in requested order"},{"category":"section","location":"multimodal/#Lazy-training-batches","page":"Multimodal data","text":"for batch in batches(ds; batchsize=32, shuffle=true, rng=MersenneTwister(42))\n    # batch.media contains bytes; decode with your image/audio library here.\n    # Train your model using decoded inputs and batch metadata.\nend\n\n# Alternatively, put decoding and collation in a per-batch callback:\nloader = batches(ds; batchsize=32, transform=b -> (\n    ids=b.id,\n    byte_lengths=length.(b.media), # replace with your actual decoder/collator\n))\n\nBatches fetch media when iterated. Your transform callback can decode images, augment samples or return model-ready arrays. Avoid collecting the iterator when the full dataset will not fit in memory.\n\nPass drop_last=true to omit a final short batch. Construct a new iterator for each shuffled epoch. An explicit RNG makes shuffle and sampling reproducible.","title":"Lazy training batches"},{"category":"section","location":"multimodal/#User-provided-embedding-models","page":"Multimodal data","text":"# A deliberately simple deterministic example, not a semantic embedding model.\nencoder(inputs) = [Float32[length(x), 1] for x in inputs]\nencoded = with_embeddings(DataFrame(id=[1,2], text=[\"cat\",\"longer text\"]), :text, encoder)\ntext_tbl = create_table(conn, \"text\", encoded)\nresult = embedding_search(text_tbl, \"cat\", encoder) |> limit(1) |> execute\n\nAn encoder receives a batch and returns one vector per input, or a matrix with one embedding per column. All embeddings must have equal, nonzero dimensions and finite values. Use the same model for stored data and queries; pass overwrite=true to replace an existing embedding column.\n\nChoose your own decoder and model packages; LanceDB.jl does not install them automatically.\n\nClose handles when finished, and remove this example's temporary database:\n\nclose(result)\nclose(text_tbl)\nclose(tbl)\nclose(conn)\nrm(path; recursive=true)\n\nThese examples execute as part of the documentation build.","title":"User-provided embedding models"},{"category":"section","location":"multimodal/#Column-marker-example","page":"Multimodal data","text":"julia> using LanceDB\n\njulia> bytes = BinaryColumn([UInt8[1, 2], UInt8[]]);\n\njulia> length.(bytes)\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 2\n 0\n\njulia> bytes[[2, 1]] isa BinaryColumn\ntrue","title":"Column marker example"},{"category":"section","location":"#LanceDB.jl","page":"Home","text":"LanceDB.jl provides Julia bindings for lancedb-c, with Tables.jl integration, vector queries, binary media columns and ID-based training batches. Julia 1.10 or later is required. No Python or native build toolchain is needed. The API is experimental and targets the lancedb-c 0.33 ABI.\n\nTables, queries and object storage\nMultimodal data and training batches\nCapabilities and ownership\nAPI reference","title":"LanceDB.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install-a-local-checkout","page":"Home","text":"From the repository root, use your existing Julia installation:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nTo use the checkout from another project, run Pkg.develop(path=\"/path/to/LanceDB.jl\") in that project's Julia environment. Install DataFrames in that environment with Pkg.add(\"DataFrames\") to run the examples below.","title":"Install a local checkout"},{"category":"section","location":"#Create-and-search-a-table","page":"Home","text":"This example is executed during the documentation build. It creates a fresh temporary database and closes its handles explicitly.\n\nusing LanceDB, DataFrames\n\nmktempdir() do path\n    open(Connection, path) do db\n        table = create_table(db, \"items\", DataFrame(\n            id = [1, 2, 3],\n            text = [\"cat\", \"dog\", \"bird\"],\n            embedding = [Float32[1, 0], Float32[0, 1], Float32[0.5, 0.5]],\n        ))\n        try\n            result = vector_search(table, Float32[1, 0], :embedding) |>\n                     limit(1) |> execute\n            try\n                rows = DataFrame(result)\n                @assert rows.id == [1]\n                println(\"Nearest item: \", only(rows.text))\n            finally\n                close(result)\n            end\n        finally\n            close(table)\n        end\n    end\nend\nNearest item: cat\n\nSmall tables can be searched without a vector index. Index training requirements depend on the selected index and configuration.","title":"Create and search a table"},{"category":"section","location":"#Work-with-an-existing-database","page":"Home","text":"open(Connection, \"./my-database\") do db\n    println(table_names(db))\n    open(Table, db, \"items\") do table\n        println(count_rows(table))\n    end\nend\n\nCreating a table with an existing name raises an error. add(table, data) appends rows; merge_insert performs keyed upserts. See Tables and queries.","title":"Work with an existing database"},{"category":"section","location":"#Resource-lifetime","page":"Home","text":"Close tables, connections, unused query builders and results when finished. Finalizers are a fallback. Executing a query consumes its builder; create a new query for another execution. Consumed expressions must be copied before reuse. See Capabilities and ownership for details.","title":"Resource lifetime"}]
}
